{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Types of Association in UML"}, {"type": "position", "content": 8}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear when talking about association in UML."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Build the macro system conceptual model following the customer requirements, through the use and interpretation of collected information, represented in class diagrams, interaction diagrams, and operation contracts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation. Exchanges simple information on programming tools and solutions to a client's requirement."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Verbs with Infinitives: Want to, in Affirmative and Negative Statements"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">want to </span>plus a verb in its infinitive form to express wishes or desires."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use <span class=\"strong\">want to </span>in affirmative statements to express there is an action you wish to perform. For affirmative sentences follow the simple present structure. Let's review it: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\"> <span class=\"strong\">Subject + want to* + verb in base form + complement.</span></div>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "*Be careful! Don't forget to add a final \u201c-s\u201d to the verb when the subject of the sentence is a third person."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">wants to</span> learn more about the associations in UML. <br/> They <span class=\"strong\">want to </span>design UML diagrams.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Now, you can also use <span class=\"strong\">want to </span>in negative statement by following the simple present structure in its negative form:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\"><div class=\"center_text\"> Subject + auxiliary (does not / do not) + want to* + verb in base form + complement.</div></span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "*Remember that when we use the auxiliary <span class=\"strong\">does not</span> or <span class=\"strong\">do not</span>, we do not conjugate the verb. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">doesn't want to </span>learn more about the associations in UML.  <br/>They <span class=\"strong\">do not </span>want to design UML diagrams.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "When you want to show different kinds of association, you use arrows; if you don't want to confuse the reader, you need to use the right arrow types."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Arrows to Denote Types of Association"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "In UML, you use <span class=\"strong\">arrows </span>to denote types of association between classes."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The types of associations that arrows can denote are the following: "}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":6,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"TYPE OF ASSOCIATION\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"EXPLANATION\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Dependency\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Two or more classes depend on each other.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Simple Association\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Classes are associated, but they do not depend on each other.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Bidirectional Association\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Dependable classes can affect one another. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Aggregation\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"There is a belonging relationship between two different classes. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Composition\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Stronger than aggregation. It denotes that A is an elemental part of class B.\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "image", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\"> enpe8n2le01ob08img01.jpg</div>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about graphics that different kinds of associations use UML."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a poster to explain the arrows used for different kinds of associations. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Include examples and images. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share the poster with the rest of the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I read different kinds of associations in UML."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I associate different kinds of associations in UML with their respective arrows. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I draw arrows for different kinds of associations in UML and I share the poster with the rest of the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "dotted-line "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a line you make using dots"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For this type of association, you use a dotted-line."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bidirectional "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that goes in two opposite directions"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If you want to use a two-way direction, you can usually use the bidirectional association for common references between class A and class B when one depends on the other."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "aggregation "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the objects you collect from one group to another"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Now, when you want class B to have objects that belong to class A, you can usually use aggregation, so classes A and B have access to each other."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "composition "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the way you join objects together"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Finally, if you want to use a similar aggregation process but you want to make more emphasis on the object of class B that belongs to class A, you can use composition by using a similar aggregation arrow."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "denote "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to show something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Okay! I want to denote my classes with arrows so I can make an association. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "arrow "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a symbol you use to give a specific direction to something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Okay! I want to denote my classes with arrows so I can make an association. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob08aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "title": "", "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n2le01ob08re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Programmer:</span> When you want to use two classes, you need to associate them so they can work together, but this depends on what kind of association you want to present using UML diagrams.\n<br /><span class=\"strong\">Assistant:</span> How can I represent those associations, then?\n<br /><span class=\"strong\">Programmer:</span> When you want to show different kinds of associations, you use arrows; plus, if you don't want to confuse the reader, you need to use the right arrow types.\n<br /><span class=\"strong\">Assistant:</span>Okay! I want to denote my classes with arrows so I can make an association. What type do I need?"}, {"time": "32000", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Programmer:</span> If you don't want to make a strict association, you can sometimes use dependency. Here, class A depends on what class B defines. If you want to change something in class B, this affects class A; that's because class B has a static method. For this type of association, you use a dotted-line. When you don't want to depend on class B, you can usually use simple associations. In other words, you can use any object from class B because class A can generally have a class B attribute. A simple association uses a common arrow that represents just one direction. If you want to use a two-way direction, you can usually use the bidirectional association for common references between class A and class B when one depends on the other."}, {"time": "83000", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Now, when you want class B to have objects that belong to class A, you can usually use aggregation, so classes A and B have access to each other. Designers can sometimes think there is no distinction between aggregation and simple association but using a different arrow. Finally, if you want to use a similar aggregation process but you want to make more emphasis on the object of class B that belongs to class A, you can use composition by using a similar aggregation arrow."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n2le01ob08re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list the different kinds of association in UML. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can mention basic characteristics for different kinds of association in UML."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can describe how to show different kinds of associations with arrows."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Why do you think it is necessary to have classes related?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What do you think you need to do when associating classes?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "